## 1. Introduction
Over past decades, with the repaid development of society, the growth of fossil fuels depletion is huge. As a result, the human has to face the emerging energy crisis, which is regarded as the bottleneck to society progress. Also, the emergent technologies are driving iterations of developing green, cheap and renewable energy storing devices. Some natural power, such as wind and water, possess favorable advantages of cleanliness and cheapness. However, the further utilization of these power is seriously hindered by their inherent characters, including intermittence shortage and difficultly-carrying. Rechargeable batteries have the merits of high energy density, extended working life and good sustainability. Thus, it has been eagerly focused by researchers, and deemed as effective strategy to tackle the mentioned energy crisis [1–9].
As potential substitutes for fossil fuels, lithium and sodium ion batteries (LIBs and SIBs) possess extensive applications, such as portable devices and electrical vehicles. However, the finite lithium resources seriously impede the further usage of LIBs, especially in the large-scale energy storing system. Taking SIBs as an alternative to LIBs is promising, because of its cheap price and sufficient natural supply. SIBs exhibit a similar working mechanism with LIBs and also induce tremendous investigations. However, the commercial graphite anode used in LIBs shows inferior electrochemical performance in SIBs, owing to the restricted ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ insertion into graphite layer [10]. Lacking of anode with high capacity blocks the acquirement of high-powered SIBs. Considering the larger radius of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ compared with $\mathrm{Li}^{+}$ $(\Gamma_{\mathrm{Na+}}\approx0.106~\mathrm{nm}$ , $\Gamma_{\mathrm{Li}+}\approx0.076~\mathrm{nm}$ ) [11], the insertion/extraction behavior of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ is more obstructed. This also is a nonnegligible obstacle for acquiring high-powered SIBs. Therefore, constructing appropriate ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ host with high capacity, superb rate performance and extended cycling life, is urgent for the application of SIBs.
Over recent years, researchers have paid considerable attentions on using metal compounds (oxides [12], phosphides [13], sulfides [14], selenides [15]) as electrodes. Because of its high theoretical capacity, the conversion-type materials of transition metal chalcogenides (TMCs) have been proverbially explored, including CoS [16], $\mathrm{FeS}_{2}$ [17], $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ [18] and $\mathrm{Ni}\mathsf{S}_{2}$ [19]. Also, this type of materials often shows better mechanical stability and higher electrical conductivity. Nevertheless, there are two primary drawbacks existed during the working of TMCs, which seriously hinders their commercialization. The first drawback is the large volume change of materials during discharge/charge procedure, leading to the structural collapse of electrode and consequently inferior cycling property. The second one is the pulverization of materials, often giving rise to the severe capacity deterioration. Previous work has confirmed that, regulating the structure and constituent of TMCs is effective in solving these issues. Prevalent strategies focusing on the morphology optimization and size reduction of TMCs can provide short diffusion path of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ . Another deeply investigated method is designing the TMCs/carbon composites. Carbon species play vital role in facilitating the electron transportation as well as buffering the volume change. Graphene is used as effective 2D conformal building blocks for TMCs, in terms of its high electronic conductivity and mechanical resilience. Xie et al. have prepared the $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ /reduced graphene oxide (RGO) nanohybrid and used it as SIBs anode. The electrode delivered exceptional cycling and rate property [20]. Zhao et al. have confined active $\mathrm{NiS_{x}}$ nanoparticles in conductive carbon nanotubes (CNTs) network, and stabilized them with polydopamine-derived carbon layer. The obtained hybrid possessed a discharge capacity above $500\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ after 200 cycles, with superb cycling stability [21]. CNTs benefited the electrical conductivity improvement, and the carbon layer suppressed volume change as well as the active materials dissolution. Superior SIBs anode of ${\mathrm{FeS}}_{2}@C$ nanocomposite was designed Liu et al [22].
Owing to its ultrahigh surface area, rich porosity and facile synthesis, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) possess application prospects in various fields. Currently, utilizing MOFs as precursor to synthesize functional target product is a growing trend. By delicately regulating its structure and composition, the obtained materials show great prospects in energy storage. Shuang et al. have fabricated carbon nanosheets embedded with $\Nu\dot{1}_{3}S_{2}$ via Ni-MOFs precursor, and utilized it as SIBs anode. After running at $0.2\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ for 100 cycles, this electrode still showed a discharge capacity of $432.8~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ [23]. By using different MOFs precursors, Xu et al. have synthesized several carbon-coated metal selenides as SIBs anode. For instance, after working at 3 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for 500 cycles, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{7}\mathrm{Se}_{8}@C$ electrode possessed a discharge capacity of $218\mathrm{\mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ [24]. Notably, the heteroatoms doping is confirmed to be promising strategy in tuning the physicochemical property of carbon species, further boosting its electrochemical performance. The facile synthesis of S-doped disordered carbon anode, which shows excellent property in SIBs, was reported by Li et al. [25]. Qin et al. have used raw corn stalks to synthesize N, P co-doped carbon material and found its superb sodium storage performance [26]. The electrochemical property of N, S co-doped mesoporous carbon was also investigated by Ye et al. [27]. Overall, the doping of heteroatoms shows positive effects, including promoting electronic conductivity and ameliorating the surface chemical functionalities.
Herein, we have reported the preparation of heteroatoms doped carbon@encapsulated $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ nanoparticles $(\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S_{2}},$ as SIBs anode, via facile polyacrylonitrile@metal organic frameworks composite-derived sulfuration strategy. The obtained ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ shows superior sodium storage performance. Specifically, under $_{0.4-3\mathrm{~V~}}$ , the ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ shows long and ultrastable cycling process at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ . After running 2095 cycles, it still presents a high discharge capacity of $546.3\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1}$ . The capacity decay ratio during the whole process is as low as $0.013\%$ per cycle. Also, it gives capacities of 540.7 and $493.6\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , even at 4 and $8\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , separately. It’s firmly believed that this work opens up new avenues for the constructing of heteroatoms doped carbon@encapsulated TMCs materials, broadening their usage in energy storage field.
