## 3.1. Characterizations for NSPCFS@CoS2
The synthetic process of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ is given in Fig. 1a. Firstly, the precursor mixture of PAN and ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{A C}})_{2}{\cdot}4{\mathsf{H}}_{2}0$ is transformed into the pink membrane of PAN- ${\mathrm{.Co}}({\mathsf{A C}})_{2}$ by the electro-spinning technique. Then, the growth of ZIF67 on its fiber surface is performed by using 2-MeIm, and the purple membrane of PAN-Co $(\mathsf{A C})_{2}@\mathsf{Z I F67}$ is obtained. After that, the generation of N-doped carbon fiber structure with embedded Co nanoparticles $({\mathsf{N P C F S@C o}})$ is conducted at high temperature. The change of metallic Co to active $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ is achieved via the sulfuration process, and the final product of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ is obtained. The generated $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ nanoparticles are well encapsulated by N, S co-doped carbon and firmly confined in the carbon fiber. The robust carbon scaffold can effectively alleviate the severe volume change of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ . ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ insertion/extraction process in ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ is clearly illustrated in Fig. 1b. The occurred electrochemical reaction is explained as follows: $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}+\mathbf{x}$ $\Nu{\sf a}^{+}+\Chi{\sf e}^{-}\to\Nu{\sf a}_{\mathrm{x}}C{\sf o}S_{2}$ . Notably, the co-doping of N and S elements and the presence of interconnected conductive network can benefit the rapid ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ transport. The digital pictures of ${\mathsf{P A N-C o}}({\mathsf{A C}})_{2}$ , P $\mathsf{A N-C o}(\mathsf{A C})_{2}@\mathsf{Z I F}67$ and ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ are given in Fig. S1. After the soaking in 2-MeIm solution, the color of membrane is changed from pink to purple.
Crystal structure of materials is investigated by XRD technique, and the obtained patterns are presented in Figs. S2 and 2a. As shown, the sample of $\mathsf{P A N-C o}(\mathsf{A C})_{2}$ shows no characteristic peaks in its pattern. After the reaction with organic ligand, ZIF67 particles are in-situ grown on the surface or inside of $\mathsf{P A N-C o}(\mathsf{A C})_{2}$ The related characteristic peaks of ZIF67 are observed in the pattern of PAN- $\mathsf{C o}(\mathsf{A C})_{2}@\mathsf{Z I F}67$ . FTIR results of PAN, PAN- ${\mathrm{.Co}}({\mathsf{A C}})_{2}$ , ZIF67, PAN$\mathrm{Co}(\mathsf{A C})_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}67$ , NSPCFS@Co and ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ are given in Fig. S2. It is obvious that, the result of $\mathrm{PAN-Co(AC)}_{2}@\mathrm{ZIF}67$ shows the whole peaks of $\mathsf{P A N-C o}(\mathsf{A C})_{2}$ and ZIF67. Obvious diffraction peaks ascribed to metallic Co are discovered after annealing. The signals at $44.3^{\circ}$ and $51.7^{\circ}$ in the pattern of NPCFS $@(0$ , belong to (111) and (200) planes of Co (JCPDS Card No. 89-4307) [28], separately. Furthermore, a broad peak at $26^{\circ}$ demonstrates the formation of graphitic carbon. Then, the sulfuration treatment is performed for NPCFS@Co. The pattern of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ is clearly given, and all new peaks are in good accordance with the reported results of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ (JCPDS card No. 41–1471). Moreover, no impurities peaks are found in the pattern of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ , indicative of its high purity. The presence of graphitic carbon is also observed. As for its FTIR results, after the sulfuration treatment, the signal at $1060~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is attributed to $\scriptstyle{C o=S}$ stretching, suggesting the formation of active $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ . The peaks at 1620 and $1167\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to the skeletal vibrations of graphitic carbon domains and $C{\mathrm{-}}C$ bond. The stretching and bending vibrations of surface water are found at around $3300{-}3500~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ [29–31].
XPS technology is used to explore the element composition of samples. The survey spectrum of $\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S_{2}}$ is presented in Fig. 2b. The emerging of O 1 s peak is attributed to the partial oxidation of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ . In Fig. 2c, the peaks at 781.8 and $798.5\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponds to Co 2p 3/2 and Co $2\mathsf{p}\ 1/2$ of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ [32], separately. The satellite peaks at around 787.0 and 803.5 eV [33], are also observed. Moreover, the peaks at 779.0 and $783.6\ \mathrm{~eV}$ manifest the existence of $C0^{2+}$ [34]. The peak at around $400~\mathrm{eV}$ in Fig. 2a directly confirms the existence of N element. The doped N atoms are rooted in the used 2-MeIm. In N 1s spectrum, the signals at 401.6, 400.3 and $399.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ are stemmed from graphitic N (N-Q), pyrrolic N (N-5) and pyridinic N (N-6) [35–37], respectively. The ratio of N-Q $(76.6\%)$ in ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ is greater than N-6 $(15.6\%)$ and N-5 $(7.8\%)$ , which signifies that the doped N atoms dominantly lie in graphical layers instead of at the periphery. As reported, the N atoms in the graphical layers facilitate the electron transport, consequently promoting its electrochemical performance of carbon species [25,38,39]. Two peaks at 163.0 and 164.0 eV coinciding with $\mathtt{S2p3}/2$ and $\mathsf{S}2\mathsf{p}\ 1/2$ , separately [40,41], are clearly shown in the S 2p spectrum. Furthermore, the signals at 165.1 and 161.7 eV illustrate the presence of $\mathsf{C}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{S}{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{C}$ bond and $S^{2-}$ ions, separately [42–44]. Another peaks associated with ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{O}}_{\mathrm{x}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathsf{C}}$ $\mathbf{\zeta}_{\mathbf{X}}=2,3$ , 4) can be found [45] in Fig. S3. In C1s spectrum, the signal at 285.5 eV confirms the existence of ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{S}}{\mathsf{-}}{\mathsf{C}}$ bond, which implies that S atoms are successfully incorporated into carbon structure [46]. In addition, the signals at 284.7 and $287.1\ \mathrm{eV}$ are ascribed to ${\mathsf{C}}{=}{\mathsf{C}}/{\mathsf{C}}{-}{\mathsf{C}}$ and $C{\mathrm{-}}\mathsf{N}$ bonds [47–53], respectively. As is well known, N and S heteroatoms possess diverse sizes and electronegativity, compared with C atoms. Incorporating these atoms into carbon framework causes the structure deformation and variation in charge density, benefiting the conductivity improvement [25,38,39]. Meanwhile, the heteroatoms doping may induce the formation of topological defects, which contributes to the enhancement of electrochemical activity.
SEM and TEM technologies are utilized to investigate the morphology, elemental composition and crystal structure of samples [54–57]. As given in Fig. 3a, the electro-spun ${\mathsf{P A N-C o}}({\mathsf{A C}})_{2}$ composite shows fiber-like morphology, with the diameter of $0.4~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ as well as smooth surface. After the growth of ZIF67, the acquired PAN- ${\mathrm{.Co}}(\mathsf{A C})_{2}$ -ZIF67 gives the rougher surface, with the increased diameter of $0.49~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . The enlarged view of PAN- ${\cdot}C_{0}({\mathsf{A C}})_{2}$ -ZIF67 in Fig. 3b shows the compactly adjacent ZIF67 particles. Elemental mapping result of PAN- ${\mathrm{.}}C_{0}(\mathsf{A C})_{2}$ -ZIF67 is shown in Fig. S4, certifying the presence of C, N, O and Co elements. TEM image of PAN-Co $(\mathsf{A C})_{2}$ -ZIF67 in Fig. $3\mathrm{g}$ also confirms the successful growth of ZIF67. Then, heat treatment under high temperature and Ar atmosphere is performed for this composite fiber to obtain the $N\mathrm{.}$ -doped porous carbon fiber structure with embedded Co nanoparticles, which is defined as NPCFS $\boldsymbol{@}\mathrm{Co}$ . Fig. 3d and e represent the SEM images of ${\mathsf{N P C F S@C o}}$ under different magnification. As clearly shown, the NPCFS@Co composite retains the fiber-like morphology well and gives slightly decreased diameter, compared with PAN-Co $(\mathsf{A C})_{2}$ -ZIF67. The direct evidence for the presence of embedded Co nanoparticles is given in Fig. 3h. Also, the porous structure is formed in NPCFS@Co. The conversion of metallic Co to active $\phantom{}C0S_{2}$ is achieved via the sulfuration treatment under high temperature, obtaining the product of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ . The corresponding SEM image is presented in Fig. 3f, verifying the well-remained fiber structure. SEM elemental mapping results of $\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S_{2}}$ are given in Fig. 4. Clearly, the homogeneously distribution of Co, S, C and N elements are found, indicative of its successful preparation. The related EDS spectrum is also obtained, showing the existence of these elements. Fig. 3i presents the related TEM image of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ . It is evident that, $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ nanoparticles are evenly and tightly embedded in the carbon framework. Previous work has confirmed that, reducing the size of active materials is an efficient strategy to promote ion transport and its wettability with electrolyte, boosting its electrochemical property. The inset picture shows the high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) image for ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ . The spacing of $0.277{\mathrm{nm}}$ between the adjoining lattice fringes is observed, corresponding to (20 0) planes of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ Additionally, the $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ nanoparticle with the size of $11\ \mathrm{\nm}$ , is well-encapsulated with derived carbon layer, which not only improves the conductivity of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ , but also alleviates the serious volume change of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ . TEM elemental mapping results are exhibited in Fig. 4. The raised white dots in the morphology (shown in Fig. 4a) represent the embedded $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ nanoparticles. Fig. 4b shows the existence of Co, S, C and N elements, which are uniformly distributed among the composite. The corresponding EDS spectrum is also given, certifying its elemental composition. The morphology of pure $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ is also presented in Fig. S5.
## 3.2. Electrochemical performance under $\ 0.1-3\ V$
Applied voltage range plays crucial part in affecting the electrochemical property of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ . Firstly, the voltage range of $0.1{-}3\mathrm{~V~}$ is employed. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) plot at $0.4~\mathrm{mV}~\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ is given in Fig. S6. In the incipient sweep, the reductive peaks at 1.10 and $1.53~\mathrm{V}$ are resulted from the $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ embedding behavior: $\mathrm{CoS}_{2}\mathrm{~+~x~Na~}^{+}+\mathrm{~x~e~}^{-}\mathrm{~\rightarrow~Na_{x}C o S}_{2}$ . The signals at 0.81, 0.70 and $0.42\mathrm{\DeltaV}$ can be attributed to the generation of solid electrolyte interface (SEI) film and the subsequent transformation behavior: $\mathsf{N a}_{\mathbf{x}}.$ - $\mathrm{CoS}_{2}+(4\mathrm{~-~}\times)\mathrm{~Na}^{+}+(4\mathrm{~-~}\times)\mathrm{~e~}^{-}\to\mathrm{Co}+2\mathrm{~Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}.$ The oxidation peaks at 1.55, 1.71 and $2.01~\mathrm{V}$ are stemmed from the formation of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ . In the later scan cycle, the reduction peaks are kept at 0.8 and $1.53\mathrm{~V~}$ , confirming the improved kinetics. Additionally, well overlapped CV curves are found for the subsequent sweep, suggesting the superb reversibility of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ . The cycling properties of bare $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ at $1\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ are given in Fig. S7. As shown, bare $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ gives poor cycling performance. Specifically, the first Coulombic efficiency is only $44.7\%$ , and an exceedingly low capacity of $57.1\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1}$ is given at 80th cycle.
Notably, ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ gives an incipient Coulombic efficiency of $87.2\%$ and a discharge capacity of $659.1\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{~h~g}^{-1}$ at 80th cycle. Cycling performances of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ at 2 and $4\mathrm{~A~g~}^{-1}$ are presented in Fig. 5a and b, separately. When running at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , the electrode delivers an incipient discharge/charge capacities of $860.2/737.6\ \mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , showing a high Coulombic efficiency of $85.7\%$ . As reported, $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ possesses theoretical specific capacity of $870.0\ \mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . Here, a high charge capacity of $737.6~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ is delivered, indicating that approximately $84.8\%\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ is utilized. Such a high utilization ratio substantiates the predominant electrochemical property of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ . When we adjust the current density to $2\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ , the discharge/charge capacities of 754.4/750.5 mA h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ are observed, reflecting a Coulombic efficiency of $99.5\%$ . Detailed discharge/charge plots at various cycles are given in Fig. 5d. Discharge plateaus at 0.5–0.6 and $0.8\mathrm{-}1.2\ \mathrm{V}$ and charge plateaus at 1.7–1.8 and $1.9\substack{-2.0\mathrm{~V~}}$ can be observed for the first cycle, well matching with CV plots. Discharge plateaus for following cycles are stabilized at 0.5–0.6 as well as $1.4\substack{-1.5\mathrm{~V}},$ and well-superimposed, illustrating the exceptional cycling stability.
Discharge/charge capacities at 30th, 50th and 100th cycles are 678.0/676.9, 682.9/681.8 and $690.2/689.6\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\mA}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\h\g}}^{-1}$ , separately. The electrode gives the capacity of $693.4\mathrm{~mA~h~g}^{-1}$ , after running 120 cycles. Also, a high capacity retention rate of $80.6\%$ is obtained. We also investigate the electrochemical property of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ at $4\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , and the related cycling plots are given in Fig. 5b and e. Obviously, the ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ shows an incipient discharge/charge capacities of 814.8/699.6 mA h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , corresponding to a Coulombic efficiency of $85.9\%$ . The specific discharge/charge capacities at 6th, 30th, 50th, 100th, 160th and 200th cycles are 607.8/592.2, 593.3/592.2, 591.1/592.2, 596.6/596.6, 603.3/602.2 and 591.1/602 $.2{\mathrm{~mA}}$ h $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , separately. After running 250 cycles, a discharge capacity of $591.1\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ is maintained, with the capacity decay ratio of $0.11\%$ per cycle. Furthermore, the nearly superposed discharge/charge plots can be found, suggesting the great cycling stability of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S_{2}}}$ . To be specific, the discharge platforms are maintained at $0.5–0.6$ and $1.4\mathrm{-}1.5\mathrm{~V~}$ excluding 1st cycle.
For the comprehensive understanding of electrochemical performance of materials, rate capability of NSPCFS ${\mathfrak{O}}\subset{\mathfrak{O}}{\mathsf{S}}_{2}$ is studied.
As given, the discharge/charge capacities at 0.1, 0.5, 1, 2 and $4\mathsf{A}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ are 841.3/714, 678.7/668.8, 666.9/661.1, 652.2/647.7 and $646.3/6$ $46.4~\mathrm{{mA}}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , separately. After switching the current rate to 8 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , the ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ gives high discharge/charge capacities of $615.8/611.3\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{~h~g^{-1}},$ , presenting a high Coulombic efficiency of $99.3\%$ . Meanwhile, a capacity retention rate of $73.2\%$ is acquired. The $\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S_{2}}$ shows a discharge capacity of $770.9\ \mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , when we turn the current rate back to $0.1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ . With the proceeding of cycling test, a discharge capacity of $833.7\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ is recovered. This demonstrates the superb reversibility of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ . The poor rate performance of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ is shown in Fig. S7. It is observed that, bare $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ shows low capacity and erratic Coulombic efficiency during test. The detailed discharge/charge plots are shown in Fig. 5f. Apparently, the discharge plateaus at 0.5, 1, 2 and $4\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ are well matched, signifying the excellent cycling stability of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ . The rate performance comparison with other reported $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ based electrodes is conducted by us. The summarized plots are given in Fig. 5h. Evidently, among these electrodes, ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ electrode gives the highest initial discharge capacity of $841.3~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ , which is approaching to its theoretical capacity. This can be attributed to our delicate structure design. Moreover, obvious capacity advantage is found at high current rate. Overall, this ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ electrode exhibits a comparative or even better rate capability than other $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ based electrodes $\mathrm{\mathrm{CoS}}_{2}@\mathrm{MCNF}$ and $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ [58], $\mathsf{N{-}}\mathsf{C/C o S_{2}}$ [59], $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ -NSCNHF [60], ${\mathsf{N S P C F@C o S}}_{2}$ [61], $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ -MCNT [62], $\cos_{2}/C$ [63], $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}/\mathsf{B N G}$ [64], $\scriptstyle\mathrm{rGO-CoS}_{2}$ -GNCS [65], $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ -GCAs [66], $\mathrm{CoS}_{2}–\mathrm{NF/rGO-NS}$ [67], $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}/\mathsf{C}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C N T}$ [68], $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}@\mathsf{M C N F}$ s [69]). We also make a contrast with other types of metal sulfides, and the corresponding curves are shown in Fig. S8. Apparently, ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ shows predominant rate property among these materials, indicative of its potential as SIBs anode candidate.
## 3.3. Electrochemical performance under 0.4–3 V
Then, we adjust the voltage range to $\phantom{-}0.4\ –3\ \vee$ , and the cycling plots at $4\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ are given in Fig. 6a. The first 5 cycles are performed at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , for the generation of protective SEI layer and activation of electrode. The initial discharge/charge capacities are 700.8 and $622.1\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1}$ , separately, showing a high Coulombic efficiency of $88.8\%$ . When we suddenly elevate the current rate to 4 ${\sf A}{\sf g}^{-1}$ , the discharge/charge capacities are 523.7 and $514.8\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ , separately, with a Coulombic efficiency of $98.3\%$ . The generation of good SEI film is responsible for this high Coulombic efficiency. The discharge/charge plots at various cycles are presented in Fig. 6c. Specific discharge/charge capacities at 20th, 60th, 100th, 150th, 200th and 320th cycles are 518.1/517.0, 514.8/513.7, 545.9/522.6, 483.7/484.4, 495.9/494.8 and $501.5/502.6~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Obviously, a capacity increment phenomenon is observed during cycling. This may be due to the activating of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ , and the gradual formation of polymeric gel-like SEI layer. Even after 400 cycles, a high discharge capacity of 530.4 mA h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ is exhibited. Compared with the initial cycle at $4\ensuremath{\mathrm{~A~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ , a slight capacity increase (about $1.3\%$ is obtained, collaborating the exceptional cycling performance of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ . During the whole process, the high Coulombic efficiency above $96\%$ and low capacity fading ratio of $0.06\%$ per cycle are observed, indicative of superior cycling stability. Evidently, the discharge/charge plots for 20th, 60th, 100th, 150th, 200th, 320th and 400 cycles are well overlapped, also proving the great cycling stability of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S_{2}}}$ . It is noteworthy that, from 200th to 400th cycle, the visible increase in discharge platform can be found, which implies the low polarization and fast reaction kinetics. Rate property under $0.4\substack{-3\mathrm{~V~}}$ of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ is given in Fig. 6b. The discharge capacities at 0.1, 0.5, 1, 2 and 4 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ are 790.0, 590.2, 568.4, 555.9 and $540.7\ \mathrm{mA}\mathrm{~h~g}^{-1}$ , separately. Even at $8\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , a discharge capacity of $493.6\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{h}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ is given, suggesting its superb rate performance. When we turn the current rate back to $0.1\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , a capacity of $589.2~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ is recovered, with a relatively high capacity preserve ratio of $74.6\%$ . This manifests the high reversibility of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ . The typical discharge/charge curves at various current rates are also given, and the partial superposition of discharge/charge plots at 0.5, 1.0 and 2.0 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ is discovered, suggesting its superior cycling stability.
Electrochemical impedance spectra (EIS) for bare $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ are clearly given in Fig. 7a and b. The Nyquist plots are composed of two partly reduplicated semicircles in the highand medium-frequency regions, following with a linear curve in the low-frequency region. Randles equivalent circuit model is utilized to analyze these plots (given in Fig. S9). $\mathtt{R_{e}}$ signifies the ohm internal resistance of materials in the cell. $\mathrm{CPE_{f}}$ and ${\sf R}_{\mathrm{f}}$ are the capacitance and resistance of the generated SEI layer, respectively. $\mathrm{CPE}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ and $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ represent the double-layer capacitance and chargetransfer resistance, separately [70]. In addition, $Z_{\mathrm{w}}$ is the Warburg impedance, related to the ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ diffusion process in electrode. Bare $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ gives $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ of 70.9, 140.6 and $134.0\ \Omega$ at 30th, 80th, and 100th cycles, separately. After 100 cycles, the increase rate for $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ is calculated to be $89.0\%$ . In striking contrast, the ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ shows lower $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ of 2.34, 1.42 and $0.81\ \Omega$ at 30th, 80th and 100th cycles, separately. The decrease in $\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ after 100 cycles can be observed for ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ . Compared with $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ , ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ shows much lower $\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ values, benefiting its exceptional sodium storage property. EIS plots and corresponding $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ values of $\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S_{2}}$ at various cycles are given in Fig. S10. $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ values at 130th, 180th, 250th, 300th and 400th cycles are 0.35, 0.88, 1.20, 1.80 and $3.08\Omega$ , respectively. Notably, after cycling at high current density (from 30th to 400th cycle), the $\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ is only increased by $31.6\%$ . This proves the good maintaining of fast ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ transfer behavior during cycling, ensuring the superb property of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ .
For the purpose of comprehensively realizing $\mathsf{N a}^{+}$ diffusion mechanism during cycling, the ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ diffusion coefficient $\left(\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Na}}^{+}\right)$ can be figured out, based on these given equations:
$$
\begin{array}{l}{\omega=2\pi f}\\ {\qquad}\\ {Z_{r e}=R_{s}+R_{c t}+R_{f}+\sigma\omega^{-1/2}}\end{array}
$$
$$
D=0.5R^{2}T^{2}/A^{2}n^{4}F^{4}C^{2}\sigma^{2}
$$
R is the gas constant, $\mathrm{\DeltaT}$ is the absolute temperature, A is the surface area of the electrode, n is the number of electrons per molecule during oxidization, F is the Faraday constant, C is the concentration of ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ , $\upsigma$ is the Warburg factor. Thus, we can acquire the $\mathbf{D_{Na}^{+}}$ values for bare $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ at various cycles. The $Z^{\prime}$ vs $\mathbf{\omega}_{\mathbf{\omega}}^{-1/2}$ curves are plotted and fitted to obtain $\upsigma$ values. $\mathbf{D_{Na}^{+}}$ for the bare $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ are $5.50~\times~10^{-16}$ , $5.79~\times~10^{-16}$ and $2.08\times10^{-15}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ s^{-1}$ at 30th, 80th and 100th cycles, separately. In striking contrast, the NSPCFS ${\mathfrak{O}}{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{o}}{\mathsf{S}}_{2}$ electrode shows obviously improved $\mathbf{D_{Na}^{+}}$ . The $\mathbf{D_{Na}^{+}}$ values are $4.57\times10^{-14}$ , $6.51\times10^{-14}$ and $1.16\times10^{-13}\mathrm{cm}^{2}s^{-1}$ at 30th, 80th and 100th cycles, respectively. It is noteworthy that, the $\mathbf{D_{Na}^{+}}$ values of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ are about two orders of magnitude higher than that value of bare $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ . The greatly improved $\mathbf{D_{Na}^{+}}$ is responsible for the superior cycling property of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ . The summarized $\mathbf{D_{Na}^{+}}$ for ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ at different cycles are given in Fig. S10. $\ifmmode\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Na}}^{\mathrm{+}}\\\ {\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Na}}^{\mathrm{+}}}\end{array}$ of NSPCFS ${\;\textcircled{a}}C0S_{2}$ at 130th, 180th, 250th, 300th and 400th cycles are $8.75\times10^{-14},$ $1.06\times10^{-13}$ , $1.88\times10^{-13}$ , $5.28\times10^{-14}$ and $1.75\times10^{-14}\mathrm{~cm}^{2}$ $s^{-1}$ , separately.
To investigate the structural stability of anode materials during discharge/charge process, we have disassembled the cells after high-rate test and used SEM as well as TEM techniques to figure out the morphology of NSPCFS $\mathsf{i}@{\mathsf{C o S}}_{2}$ electrode. Fig. 8a and b show the TEM images of $\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S_{2}}$ under different magnification, and it is evident that the fiber-like morphology is well retained and becomes much rougher, after high-rate test. Also, the black dots, representing active $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ , are tightly confined in carbon framework, instead of dropping out. Other smooth fiber near ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ is glass fiber (GF) separator. The HRTEM result is given in Fig. 8c, and the obvious spacing of $0.277\ \mathrm{nm}$ between the adjoining lattice fringes is attributed to (2 0 0) plane of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ . The existence of Co, S, C and N elements is found in EDS spectrum. Besides, the Al, Si signals are derived from GF separator, and the peaks of Na, O are resulted from the residue of sodium salt as well as ester-based electrolyte. SEM images under different magnification, are presented in Fig. 8e and f. ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ after high-rate test gives good preserve of fiber-like morphology. Additionally, its surface gets much rougher, in comparison to pristine fiber surface, which may be due to the generation of SEI film.
## 3.4. Long term cycling performance
The long term cycling performance of $\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S_{2}}$ is also explored in this work. The voltage range is fixed at $0.4\substack{-3\mathrm{~V~}}$ and the current rate is $0.1-1\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ . The corresponding cycling curves are exhibited in Fig. 9a. As shown, the ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ shows an incipient discharge/charge capacities of $751.5/666.1\ \mathrm{mAh}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , separately, acquiring a Coulombic efficiency of $88.6\%$ . Obviously, the electrode remains steady and high capacity upon the whole 2095 cycles. Specifically, the discharge/charge capacities at 100th, 300th, 500th, 1000th, 1500th and 2000th cycles are 560.3/562.0, 568.6/569.6, 565.4/566.3, 564.6/566.3, 562.5/563.8 and 556.9/556 mA h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. After running 2095 cycles, the electrode still shows a high capacity of $546.3~\mathrm{mA}$ h $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ . The capacity retention ratio is reckoned to be $72.7\%$ . Notably, an exceedingly low capacity fading ratio of $0.013\%$ per cycle can be achieved. Moreover, the Coulombic efficiency for the whole cycling process is remained above $99.0\%$ (except for the 1st cycle). These results indicate the superb cycling performance of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ , which profits from its unique architecture in several aspects, such as heteroatoms doping, interconnected structure and conductive fiber framework. Specific discharge/charge plots are depicted in Fig. 9b, and well-overlapped discharge or charge platforms can be found, proving its superior cycling stability.
EIS plots for bare $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ and ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ during this long term cycling process are given in Figs. 10 and S11. The fitted EIS curves for bare $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ are also given in Fig. 10. As discussed above, $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ values can be calculated based on Randles equivalent circuit model. Bare $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ shows $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ of 144.2, 189.6 and $215.7\ \Omega$ at 50th, 80th and 100th cycles, separately. By contrast, the NSPCF $S@C o S_{2}$ gives lower $\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ of 4.27, 2.28 and $1.73\ \Omega$ at 50th, 80th and 100th cycles, separately. Interestingly, after 100 cycles, the decreased $\mathbb{R}_{\mathrm{ct}}$ is observed. Compared with $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ , ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ electrode shows remarkably reduced $\mathtt{R_{c t}}.$ . Additionally, $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ values for ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ at different cycles are summarized in Fig. S11. Specifically, $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ values at 30th, 130th, 900th, 1100th, 1300th, 1754th, 1939th and 2039th cycles are 2.49, 1.30, 3.22, 3.63, 2.23, 2.34, 3.29 and $5.81\ \Omega$ , separately. Even after such a long term cycling (from 30th to 2039th cycles), the $\mathtt{R_{c t}}$ is merely increased by $133\%$ . This can be attributed to the superb structural stability of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ , as discussed above. $\mathbf{D_{Na}^{+}}$ values of electrodes during this long term cycling process is also calculated. The associated $z$ vs $\mathbf{\omega}_{\mathbf{\omega}}^{-1/2}$ curves are plotted in Fig. 10d and e. Bare $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ exhibits $\mathbf{D_{Na}^{+}}$ of $5.50\times10^{-16}$ , $5.80\times10^{-16}$ and $2.08\times10^{-15}\mathrm{cm}^{2}s^{-1}$ at 50th, 80th and 100th cycles, separately. The corresponding $\mathbf{D_{Na}^{+}}$ are $1.72\times10^{-14}$ , $1.94\times10^{-14}$ and $4.20\times10^{-14}\ \mathrm{\overline{{c}}m}^{2}\ s^{-1}$ at 50th, 80th and 100th cycles, respectively. It is evident that, $\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S_{2}}$ shows markedly promoted $\mathbf{D_{Na}^{+}}$ values, in comparison to $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ . The summarized $\mathbf{D_{Na}^{+}}$ for $\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S_{2}}$ at various cycles are given in Fig. S11. $\ifmmode\mathrm{D}_{\mathrm{Na}}^{\mathrm{+}}\qquad$ of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ at 30th and 130th cycles are $2.98\times10^{-14}$ and $5.22\times10^{-14}\mathrm{cm}^{2}s^{-1}$ . Moreover, $\mathbf{D_{Na}^{+}}$ values at 900th, 1100th, 1300th, 1754th, 1939th and 2039th cycles are calculated to be $8.45\times10^{-14}$ , $3.18\times10^{-14},$ , $2.50\times10^{-14}$ , $6.67\times10^{-14}$ , $8.45\times10^{-14}$ and $1.14\times10^{-14}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}$ $s^{-1}$ , respectively.
SEM and TEM testes are also employed to study the structural stability of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ after long term cycling test (2095 cycles). The related images are given in Fig. 11. The retention of fiber-like morphology of $\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S_{2}}$ after 1000 cycles is found in Fig. S12. Even after 2095 cycles, the favorable preserve of fiberlike morphology is also clearly observed, with rougher surface. This can be ascribed to the presence of SEI film. The adjacent lattice fringes distance of $0.277~\mathrm{{nm}}$ in HRTEM image, belongs to (002) plane of $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ , demonstrating that the crystal structure of active $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ can be well retained, rather than destroyed, during such a long working period. This may explain the achievement of exceptional cycling property. The positive effect of this interconnected N, S co-doped carbon framework can be explained as follows: Firstly, its high conductivity character benefits the fast ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ and electron diffusion; Secondly, this robust scaffold can efficiently alleviate the volume change of confined $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ upon cycling, thus improving the structural stability of electrode. SEM images under different magnification, given in Fig. 11c and d, also prove the good retention of materials structure.
## 3.5. $N a^{+}$ reaction kinetics and storage mechanism
Based on CV curves at different scan rates, the ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ reaction kinetics and storage mechanism of ${\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S}}_{2}$ electrode can be deeply explored, and the resultant plots are depicted in Fig. 12a. The whole area under CV plots represents the total energy storage, arising from Faradaic and non-Faradaic behaviors [71]. Faradaic behavior is composed of a redox pseudocapacitive reaction and a diffusion-controlled reaction. In Fig. S13, the nonlinear dependent relation between peak current (i) and square root of scan rate $(\mathbf{v}^{1/2})$ can be observed, proving the co-presence of Faradaic and non-Faradaic behaviors during ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ embedding/removing reactions. The specific contribution value of capacitive effect and diffusion-controlled effect on the whole energy storage, can be calculated on the basis of CV curves. The relation between peak current (i) and scan rate $(\boldsymbol{\mathsf{v}})$ is explained by following formulas.
$$
l o g(i)=b l o g(\upsilon)+l o g(a)
$$
Here, $a$ and $b$ are the tunable parameters. When b is close to 1, the electrochemical process is controlled by pseudocapacitance reaction. When b is approaches to 0.5, the electrochemical process is dominated by diffusion reaction. The curves of log(i) versus log (v) at peaks 1 to 3, giving the slopes (b values) of 0.704, 0.553 and 0.712, respectively. These values suggest the existence of pseudocapacitive behavior. The precise pseudocapacitance contribution proportion at fixed potential is determined according to below formulas:
$$
i(V)=k_{1}\nu+k_{2}\nu^{1/2}
$$
$$
i(V)/\nu^{1/2}=k_{1}\nu^{1/2}+k_{2}
$$
Here, $\mathsf{i}(\mathsf{V}),\mathsf{k}_{1}\mathsf{v}$ and ${\bf k}_{2}{\bf v}^{1/2}$ represent the whole current at fixed potentials, current from pseudocapacitance contribution, and current from the diffusion contribution, separately [72]. The curves of $\mathrm{i}(\mathsf{V})/\mathsf{v}^{1/2}\ \nu s\ \mathsf{v}^{1/2}$ at various potentials can be depicted, and $\mathbf{k}_{1}$ as well as $\mathbf{k}_{2}$ values are the fitted slope and intercept values, separately. Fig. 12c gives the detailed-fitting curve for pseudocapacitive contribution (dark yellow region) at $0.4\:\mathrm{mV}\:s^{-1}$ , and the proportion is calculated to be $61.3\%$ . In addition, the pseudocapacitive contribution proportion are elevated to $66.5\%$ at $0.6\mathrm{\mV\}s^{-1}$ , $70.6\%$ at $0.8\mathrm{\mV\}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ , $73.5\%$ at $1.0\:\mathrm{mV}\:s^{-1}$ and $79.2\%$ at $2.0\ \mathrm{mV}\ s^{-1}$ , respectively, as shown in Fig. 12d. The relatively-high pseudocapacitance contribution proportions suggest the domination of pseudocapacitance reaction, facilitating the promotion of rate property. The result of rate test also confirms this conclusion.
## 4. Conclusion
In conclusion, a superior heteroatoms doped carbon@encapsulated cobalt sulfide $({\mathrm{NSPCFS@CoS_{2}}})$ anode for SIBs is successfully designed and synthesized via a facile polyacrylonitrile@metal organic frameworks composite-derived sulfuration strategy. This electrode delivers steady cycling property at high current density and excellent rate performance, which can be attributed to its delicate structure design. For instance, when the voltage of $0.4\substack{-3\mathrm{~V~}}$ is used, it shows a discharge capacity of $546.3\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\h\g}^{-1}$ after 2095 cycles at $1\mathrm{Ag}^{-1}$ , showing an exceedingly low capacity fading rate of $0.013\%$ per cycle. Moreover, high discharge capacities of 540.7 and $493.6\mathrm{mAhg}^{-1}$ are delivered, when the current rate is elevated to 4 and $8{\mathrm{~A~g}}^{-1}$ , respectively. Compared with other $\mathsf{C o S}_{2}$ -based electrodes, $\mathsf{N S P C F S@C o S_{2}}$ electrode gives the highest initial discharge capacity of $841.3\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{h}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ at $0.1\mathrm{~A~g}^{-1}$ . Also, this electrode gives better electrochemical property at high current rate. The deeply investigations on ${\mathsf{N a}}^{+}$ reaction kinetics and storage mechanism are conducted. We strongly believe that this facile and reasonable strategy can provides efficient inspiration towards constructing of heteroatoms doped carbon@encapsulated TMCs materials, broadening their usage in energy storing field.
